// JCTC20, transcribed from Pugh's Causality Test Cases:
//
//   Thread 1:          Thread 2:        Thread 3:
//   join thread 3      r2 = y           r3 = x
//   r1 = x             x = r2           if (r3 = 0)
//   y = r1                                x = 42
//
// Thread 1 waits for thread 3 to finish before it reads x, and runs at the same
// time as thread 2. Pugh: "the same as test case 18, except that thread 1 has
// been split into two threads" -- thread 3 is the first half of test 18's
// thread 1, moved onto a thread of its own, and the join puts thread 1's second
// half back after it.
//
// MoRDor has no join statement, but a parallel block followed by statements is
// joined (#81). So thread 3 is a parallel block inside thread 1's branch, with
// `{ skip }` as its other thread, and thread 1's body follows the block. Thread 2
// stays outside it, concurrent with both.
//
// Two earlier encodings got the join wrong in opposite directions, and are
// recorded on #49 and #51. The original put thread 1's body after a block
// holding threads 2 and 3, so thread 1 also waited for thread 2. 31e57c9 made all
// three threads concurrent, which dropped the join altogether.
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  // Thread 2
  r2 := y;
  x := r2
} ||| {
  {
    // Thread 3
    r3 := x;
    if (r3 = 0) { x := 42 } else { skip }
  } ||| {
    skip
  };
  // Thread 1, after joining thread 3
  r1 := x;
  y := r1
}
%%
allow (r1 = 42 && r2 = 42 && r3 = 42) [JMM] "Allowed. This is the same as test case 18, except that thread 1 has been split into two threads."
